{
  "Answer": {
    "Title": "Parallel nodes ignored the test split",
    "Explanation": [
      "Parallelism on the `test` job is set to 2. The log confirms two nodes ran (CIRCLE_NODE_TOTAL=2). `circleci tests split` did produce a split list on each node: node 0 got src/test_goodbye.sh and node 1 got src/test_hello.sh. That list was only written to /tmp/split.txt and never used. The loop `for f in src/test_*.sh` iterates over the full glob, so both nodes ran test_goodbye.sh and test_hello.sh. The suite was therefore not actually split: each node ran every file, the suite ran twice in total, and the RAN (2) vs EXPECTED (1) check failed on both nodes with exit 1.",
      "",
      "The fix is to drive the loop from the `circleci tests split` output, e.g. `for f in $(cat /tmp/split.txt)`. Each node then runs only its assigned file, the two nodes together cover the suite exactly once, RAN equals EXPECTED, and the job goes green with no workflow change. The other options do not work:",
      "- Raising parallelism alone would still run the full suite on every node.",
      "- Splitting the work into separate workflow jobs violates the no-workflow-change constraint.",
      "- Dropping parallelism removes the required split."
    ],
    "ActualAnswer": [
      "{",
      "  \"each_node_ran_full_suite\": true,",
      "  \"fix\": \"use_circleci_tests_split\",",
      "  \"suite_split\": false",
      "}"
    ],
    "ExpectedAnswer": [
      [
        "{",
        "  \"each_node_ran_full_suite\": true,",
        "  \"fix\": \"use_circleci_tests_split\",",
        "  \"suite_split\": false",
        "}"
      ]
    ],
